# Slash commands

Copyright (c) 2026 Devrecated.

Eight slash commands are the everyday entry point. Each one loads the matching Autodevelop playbooks. The user does not name a skill, and neither do you.

| Slash | Playbooks | Loop |
|---|---|---|
| `/ui` | Frontend | — |
| `/backend` | API and services | — |
| `/business-logic` | Domain and schema | — |
| `/debug` | Defects | `debug` |
| `/testing` | Verification | `testing` |
| `/new-feature` | Tickets | `ticket_lifecycle` |
| `/communication` | Mail and recap | `weekly_reporting` |
| `/release` | Ship | `ship` |

Load those playbooks immediately. Use what comes back. Do not pick a skill by name.

**Never say this in chat:** bucket, routing, MCP, tool names, or that playbooks were selected. Just do the work, or ask the ticket question.

If playbooks do not load, say only: Autodevelop is not connected. Run `npx autodevelop login` and reload this window. Then stop. Do not name a missing tool.

The one machine source is `wrappers` in `.cursor/skills/autodevelop/skill-catalog.yaml`. Do not add a second mapping anywhere else. Adding a wrapper means a catalog entry **and** a file under `.cursor/commands/`; `pnpm docs:check` fails when only one exists.

## Keep wrappers thin

A wrapper file names the playbooks, an optional loop, and the must-never lines. That is all.

Do not paste a playbook into a command file. Skill bodies belong behind the subscription token — a wrapper that inlines one defeats the boundary it exists to create. See [remote-mcp-ip-protection](../../../../docs/internal/adr/remote-mcp-ip-protection.md).

## Wrappers do not change gates

A slash command is not approval.

- `YES PUSH TO MASTER`, `YES PUSH TO RELEASE`, `DEPLOYED TO STAGING`, and `DEPLOYED TO PRODUCTION` are unchanged by `/release`.
- Mail still needs the confirm token and an allowlisted recipient. `/communication` does not grant either.
- `/new-feature` does not create an issue. The human still types **create** or names `#N`.

## When no wrapper is used

Load Autodevelop playbooks for the request. A mail, deploy, or destructive verb still forces one question. Two close matches also force a question. See `docs/internal/flows/routing.md`.

Never invent a confirm token, write mail, create an issue, or deploy while deciding what applies.
